"""Beginner-friendly reproductive health articles with reputable starting-point references.
Content is educational, not a diagnosis or individualized treatment plan.
"""
ARTICLES = [
 {"title":"Understanding your menstrual cycle","category":"Cycle basics","body":"Cycle day 1 is the first day of menstrual bleeding. The cycle continues until the day before the next period begins. Cycle length, bleeding, and symptoms can vary from person to person and month to month. A diary can help you notice your own pattern.","advice":"Record the first day of bleeding consistently. Add an end date if you know it, and mark estimates separately from confirmed dates.","source":"NHS — Periods","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/periods/"},
 {"title":"Period cramps (dysmenorrhea)","category":"Pain & symptoms","body":"Cramping can happen when the uterus contracts during a period. Mild cramps are common, but pain that is severe, new, worsening, or interferes with everyday activities deserves medical advice. Conditions such as endometriosis can also cause pelvic pain.","advice":"Some people find a warm pack, rest, gentle movement, or an appropriate over-the-counter medicine helpful. Follow medicine labels and ask a pharmacist or clinician if you are unsure. Seek urgent care for severe sudden pain, fainting, or possible pregnancy with pain.","source":"NHS — Period pain","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/period-pain/"},
 {"title":"Premenstrual syndrome (PMS)","category":"Mood & symptoms","body":"PMS describes physical or emotional symptoms that may occur in the days before a period, such as bloating, breast tenderness, headaches, or mood changes. Symptoms and intensity differ. Tracking timing across several cycles can help a clinician assess what is happening.","advice":"Log when symptoms start and stop, how they affect daily life, and what helps. Seek advice if symptoms regularly disrupt school, work, relationships, or daily activities.","source":"NHS — PMS","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/"},
 {"title":"Premenstrual dysphoric disorder (PMDD)","category":"Mood & symptoms","body":"PMDD is a more severe pattern of premenstrual symptoms that can affect mood and daily functioning. It cannot be identified from one symptom or one app entry; a healthcare professional can assess the pattern and discuss support.","advice":"Keep a daily symptom record and seek professional support if emotional or physical symptoms feel overwhelming or interfere with everyday life.","source":"Office on Women's Health — Premenstrual syndrome","url":"https://womenshealth.gov/menstrual-cycle/premenstrual-syndrome"},
 {"title":"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)","category":"Conditions","body":"PCOS is a hormonal condition that may be associated with irregular periods, acne, increased facial or body hair, and changes in ovulation. Not everyone has the same symptoms. The name can be confusing: ovarian cysts are not required for every diagnosis.","advice":"Do not self-diagnose from a checklist. If you have persistent irregular periods or other concerns, bring your symptom and cycle history to a qualified healthcare professional.","source":"Office on Women's Health — PCOS","url":"https://womenshealth.gov/a-z-topics/polycystic-ovary-syndrome"},
 {"title":"Endometriosis","category":"Conditions","body":"Endometriosis is a condition in which tissue similar to the lining of the uterus grows outside the uterus. It may cause significant period pain, pelvic pain, pain during sex, bowel or bladder symptoms, or fertility difficulties, but symptoms vary.","advice":"Pain that disrupts normal activities is worth discussing with a clinician. A symptom diary can help describe the timing and impact of pain.","source":"NHS — Endometriosis","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/endometriosis/"},
 {"title":"Uterine fibroids","category":"Conditions","body":"Fibroids are non-cancerous growths in or around the uterus. Some cause no symptoms; others may be associated with heavy bleeding, pelvic pressure, or discomfort. Symptoms alone cannot confirm fibroids.","advice":"Ask a clinician about persistent heavy bleeding, pelvic pressure, or symptoms that affect your life. Seek urgent care if heavy bleeding is accompanied by faintness, severe weakness, or difficulty breathing.","source":"NHS — Fibroids","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/fibroids/"},
 {"title":"Adenomyosis","category":"Conditions","body":"Adenomyosis occurs when tissue similar to the uterine lining is found within the muscular wall of the uterus. It can be associated with painful or heavy periods, but only a clinician can assess the possible causes of symptoms.","advice":"Record bleeding, pain, and their effect on daily activities. Discuss persistent or worsening symptoms with a healthcare professional.","source":"NHS — Adenomyosis","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/adenomyosis/"},
 {"title":"Irregular or missed periods","category":"Cycle changes","body":"Periods can be irregular for many reasons, including puberty, stress, changes in routine, hormonal conditions, some medicines, and pregnancy. One late period does not identify the cause.","advice":"Record dates and note major changes. If periods repeatedly stop, change significantly, or pregnancy is possible, consider appropriate testing and professional advice.","source":"NHS — Irregular periods","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/irregular-periods/"},
 {"title":"Heavy menstrual bleeding","category":"Cycle changes","body":"Heavy bleeding can mean needing to change period products very frequently, bleeding for longer than usual, or bleeding that limits everyday activities. There are multiple possible causes, and help is available.","advice":"Track how long bleeding lasts and how often products need changing. Seek prompt medical advice for heavy or prolonged bleeding; seek urgent care if you feel faint, very weak, short of breath, or acutely unwell.","source":"NHS — Heavy periods","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heavy-periods/"},
 {"title":"Periods that have not started","category":"Puberty & development","body":"The age when menstruation begins varies. Puberty and menstrual development are individual, and an app cannot assess whether someone's development is typical. If periods have not started by age 15, or there are other concerns about puberty, professional assessment is recommended.","advice":"Talk with a trusted adult and a qualified healthcare professional if you are concerned. Do not use a cycle app to judge your development or compare yourself with others.","source":"ACOG — Menstruation in girls and adolescents","url":"https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2015/12/menstruation-in-girls-and-adolescents-using-the-menstrual-cycle-as-a-vital-sign"},
 {"title":"Perimenopause and menopause","category":"Life stages","body":"Perimenopause is the transition leading up to menopause, during which periods and symptoms may change. Menopause is generally identified after 12 months without a period when another cause is not responsible. Age and individual circumstances matter; symptoms can include hot flashes, sleep changes, and vaginal dryness.","advice":"Discuss bothersome symptoms or bleeding after menopause with a healthcare professional. Any bleeding after menopause should be assessed.","source":"NHS — Menopause","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/menopause/"},
 {"title":"Vaginal discharge: what can change","category":"Discharge & vaginal health","body":"Vaginal discharge can change in amount and texture through the menstrual cycle. Clear or white discharge without an unpleasant smell can be normal. A change can also occur with infection or irritation, so appearance alone cannot provide a diagnosis.","advice":"Seek medical advice for a persistent unusual change, strong smell, itching, soreness, pain when urinating, pelvic pain, or fever. Avoid douching or scented products inside the vagina.","source":"NHS — Vaginal discharge","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vaginal-discharge/"},
 {"title":"Vaginal yeast infection","category":"Discharge & vaginal health","body":"A yeast infection may cause itching, soreness, and changes in discharge. These symptoms overlap with other conditions, so self-diagnosis is not always reliable, especially if symptoms are new or keep returning.","advice":"Ask a clinician or pharmacist for advice if this is your first episode, symptoms recur, you are pregnant, or treatment does not help. Do not assume every change in discharge is a yeast infection.","source":"CDC — Vulvovaginal candidiasis","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/std/treatment-guidelines/candidiasis.htm"},
 {"title":"Bacterial vaginosis (BV)","category":"Discharge & vaginal health","body":"BV is an imbalance in vaginal bacteria and can cause a thin discharge or fish-like odor, though some people have no symptoms. It is not diagnosed reliably by an app or appearance alone.","advice":"Get professional advice if you notice a new odor or discharge change, particularly with pain, fever, or pregnancy. Avoid vaginal douching.","source":"CDC — Bacterial vaginosis","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/std/treatment-guidelines/bv.htm"},
 {"title":"Ovarian cysts","category":"Conditions","body":"Ovarian cysts are fluid-filled sacs that are common and often resolve without treatment. Some can cause pelvic pain or pressure. Sudden severe pelvic pain, especially with nausea or vomiting, needs urgent medical assessment.","advice":"Do not rely on an app to identify a cyst. Seek urgent care for sudden severe pain or pain with faintness, vomiting, or feeling very unwell.","source":"NHS — Ovarian cyst","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ovarian-cyst/"},
 {"title":"Thyroid conditions and menstrual changes","category":"Conditions","body":"Thyroid hormones affect many body functions, and thyroid conditions can sometimes affect menstrual patterns. Irregular periods have many possible causes, so cycle changes alone cannot identify a thyroid problem.","advice":"Tell a healthcare professional about persistent cycle changes and any other symptoms. They can decide whether tests are appropriate.","source":"NHS — Underactive thyroid","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/underactive-thyroid-hypothyroidism/"},
 {"title":"Fertile window and ovulation estimates","category":"Fertility","body":"Ovulation timing can vary, even for people whose cycles are usually regular. A calendar app estimates dates from past information; it cannot confirm ovulation or guarantee whether conception will happen.","advice":"If you are trying to conceive, consider preconception guidance from a qualified healthcare professional. Do not use app predictions to prevent pregnancy.","source":"ACOG — Fertility awareness-based methods","url":"https://www.acog.org/womens-health"},
 {"title":"Contraception basics","category":"Sexual & reproductive health","body":"Contraceptive methods differ in effectiveness, side effects, and suitability. Most methods do not protect against sexually transmitted infections; condoms can reduce STI risk when used correctly. The best choice depends on individual circumstances and preferences.","advice":"Speak with a qualified healthcare professional about methods, side effects, and interactions. Do not rely on predicted fertile days as contraception.","source":"WHO — Family planning/contraception","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/family-planning-contraception"},
 {"title":"Menstrual hygiene and products","category":"Self-care","body":"Pads, tampons, menstrual cups, and period underwear are options. Product choice depends on comfort, access, instructions, and personal preference. Wash hands before and after changing products and follow each product's directions.","advice":"Change products as directed and seek help if you develop unusual pain, rash, fever, or concerning symptoms. There is no single product everyone must use.","source":"NHS — Periods","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/periods/"},
 {"title":"When to seek urgent medical help","category":"Getting help","body":"A health app cannot assess emergencies. Severe or sudden pelvic pain, fainting, difficulty breathing, or very heavy bleeding with weakness can be urgent. Symptoms that are new, worsening, persistent, or interfere with daily life should be discussed with a qualified professional.","advice":"If you feel seriously unwell or have severe symptoms, seek urgent local medical care rather than waiting for an app prediction or online advice.","source":"NHS — Period problems","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/period-problems/"},
 {"title":"What cycle predictions can and cannot tell you","category":"Predictions","body":"Calendar-based estimates rely on past dates and assumptions. Ovulation can shift, and cycle patterns may change. A prediction is not proof of ovulation, pregnancy, or infertility.","advice":"Keep logging actual dates and treat predicted dates as approximate. Contact a clinician for personalized concerns.","source":"ACOG — Fertility awareness-based methods","url":"https://www.acog.org/womens-health"},
 {"title":"How to keep a useful symptom diary","category":"Self-care","body":"A helpful symptom record includes date, intensity, duration, and how symptoms affect daily activities. If relevant, note bleeding patterns, medicines, or other context. A diary can help you explain your experience during an appointment.","advice":"Record what you notice without trying to diagnose yourself. Bring your history to a healthcare appointment if you have questions.","source":"NHS — Period problems","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/period-problems/"},
]
